From 746e028880017327f1b9239f410835598e3322f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aidan <eltorago@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 06:15:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] patches: track which have been applied, by content
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The device bridge refuses to touch a .git directory, so a session cannot ask git on
the work machine what has been applied. tools/patch_status.py answers it from the
working files: every patch records the SHA-256 of each file it leaves behind and of
each file as it was before, so a copy is read against the series rather than against
a date or a size.

Four things it had to learn to say, each after saying something false:

- A file that is simply not in the copy being checked is not a mismatch, and calling
  it partial is a false alarm.
- A file the patch creates, absent from the copy, is not a gap either. It is evidence
  the patch has not been applied.
- A patch series is linear, so the answer is a depth, not a verdict per patch.
  tests/injected.py is touched by patch 1 and again by patch 2, so once both are
  applied it matches patch 2 and not patch 1 — asked on its own, patch 1 reads as
  edited since. A file is now compared against the newest patch at or below the depth
  being tested that touches it.
- And a .patch file is transport rather than source, as is the manifest beside it:
  tracking the manifest means every series ends with a commit describing the series
  before it. Both travel with the delivery instead, which is where a later session
  looks for them, since this container is reclaimed when the session ends and the
  work machine is the archive.

Checked against the work machine: nothing applied, and every file that exists there
matches its pre-patch fingerprint, so nothing has been hand-edited.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
